Define _X_NONSTRING to nothing.

XKBproto.h and XKBstr.h use it in the position

    CARD8	name[XkbKeyNameLength]	_X_NONSTRING;

and Xfuncproto.h defines it as __attribute__((nonstring)) when
__has_attribute(nonstring) answers true, which clang's does.  clang accepts
that attribute in neither position a C declarator can hold -- after the array
bound, as here:

    XKBproto.h:679:23: error: expected ';' at end of declaration list

or in front of the declarator name:

    XKBproto.h:703:10: error: duplicate member '_X_NONSTRING'

so xorg-server does not compile.  GCC takes the trailing form; clang has no
position for it.

The attribute is a diagnostic hint: it tells the compiler that the array is not
NUL-terminated, so that a strlen or a strcpy of it is warned about.  Nothing in
the struct layout, the field name, the type or the XKB protocol depends on it,
and the six declarations that use it are read as fixed-length key names, not as
strings.  Dropping it costs a missing warning on code that does not exist here.

Defining it to nothing rather than editing the six declarations is the smaller
change and the one that cannot go stale when the headers are updated.
diff -urN porig/xorgproto-2024.1/include/X11/Xfuncproto.h ptest/xorgproto-2024.1/include/X11/Xfuncproto.h
--- a/include/X11/Xfuncproto.h
+++ b/include/X11/Xfuncproto.h
@@ -207,10 +207,6 @@
 
 /* Mark a char array/pointer as not containing a NUL-terminated string */
 /* requires xproto >= 7.0.33 */
-#if __has_attribute(nonstring)
-# define _X_NONSTRING __attribute__((nonstring))
-#else
-# define _X_NONSTRING
-#endif
+#define _X_NONSTRING
 
 #endif /* _XFUNCPROTO_H_ */
